17. OPEN-WEIGHT AI — ACTUAL TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION
==================================================

The challenge requires a meaningful open-source/open-weight AI contribution.

Use a real Gemma model through a supported inference provider.

Do not use a closed model as a silent replacement.

Build a modular server-side model adapter.

REQUIRED MODEL CAPABILITIES:
- Analyse extracted project content.
- Identify project purpose.
- Extract evidence-backed findings.
- Classify verified information and hypotheses.
- Identify unknowns.
- Suggest unfinished features with supporting evidence.
- Generate a revival roadmap.
- Return structured output suitable for the interface.

TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- Keep API keys in server-side environment secrets.
- Never expose credentials in browser JavaScript.
- Validate model responses.
- Handle timeouts, errors, rate limits, and invalid output.
- Limit input size.
- Select relevant source excerpts rather than sending arbitrary oversized repositories.
- Keep inference cost and latency under control.
- Display the real model identifier and provider.
- Provide an actual connection test.
- Clearly disclose when a model is not configured.

If the model is unavailable, the preloaded sample must still be explorable.

Sample data must be clearly identified as precomputed demonstration data.

Do not simulate real AI inference with a fake loading animation.

Do not claim that model weights run locally if inference happens on a hosted service.

Include a README section documenting:
- Model name and version.
- Model source.
- Inference provider.
- Integration architecture.
- Relevant model terms.
- Limitations.
- How to configure the endpoint and credentials.

Do not claim that the entire application is open source simply because it uses an open-weight model.

